5417] [17592186414635 #edb/ref 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$a\\le -1\\text{ or }b=0$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a\\le -1\\text{ or }b=0) & ≡¬(a\\le -1∨b=0) \\\\  & ≡¬(a\\le -1)∧¬(b=0) \\\\  & ≡a>-1∧b\\ne 0 \\\\  & ≡a>-1\\text{ and }b\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414636 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414636 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414636 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414636 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is not a prime number, or $x$ is odd.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is a prime number\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is an odd integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414636 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a099938-e369-51af-98a6-9db474374a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414636 #edb/ref 1074 "The sentence \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not a prime number.\n\n can be expressed symbolically as $¬P.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as \n\n$$\n¬P∨Q.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414637 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414637 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414637 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414637 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is not an irrational number, or $x$ is not a complex number.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following predicates:\n\n- $Y(x):$ $x$ is an irrational number\n\n- $Z(x):$ $x$ is a complex number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414637 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31bb631f-458f-5c01-a798-8fd4daa7d0e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414637 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's break our sentence down into individual statements:\n\n- The sentence \"$x$ is not an irrational number\" can be expressed symbolically as $¬Y.$\n\n- The sentence \"$x$ is not a complex number\" can be expressed symbolically as $¬Z.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as \n\n$$\n¬Y∨¬Z.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414638 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414638 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414638 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414638 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is not positive, or $x$ is not odd.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is positive\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is odd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414638 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "389aeeff-ffb8-5897-88a3-b12029287eb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414638 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's break our sentence down into individual statements:\n\n- The sentence \"$x$ is not positive\" can be expressed symbolically as $¬P.$\n\n- The sentence \"$x$ is not odd\" can be expressed symbolically as $¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as \n\n$$\n¬P∨¬Q.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $n$  is prime and  $m$  is even.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "501a9cd9-7487-5eca-998b-73598c18e09d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414639 #edb/ref 1074 "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$n$ is prime and $m$ is even\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(n\\text{ is prime and }m\\text{ is even}) & ≡¬(n\\text{ is prime}∧m\\text{ is even}) \\\\  & ≡¬(n\\text{ is prime})∨¬(m\\text{ is even}) \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not prime}∨m\\text{ is odd} \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not prime or }m\\text{ is odd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is an integer or $x$ is irrational.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52356a50-945f-5f86-892e-4a577c3252b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414640 #edb/ref 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$ is an integer or $x$ is irrational\" is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is an integer or }x\\text{ is irrational }) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is an integer}∨x\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is an integer})∧¬(x\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not an integer}∧x\\text{ is not irrational } \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not an integer and }x\\text{ is not irrational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is rational and $y$ is irrational. \n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60b90c12-945a-57da-a90e-2ce25715910b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414641 #edb/ref 1074 "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$ is rational and $y$ is irrational\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is rational and }y\\text{ is irrational}) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is rational}∧y\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is rational})∨¬(y\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not rational}∨y\\text{ is not irrational} \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not rational or }y\\text{ is not irrational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414642 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414642 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414642 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414642 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is neither divisible by $2$ nor divisible by $3.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $Q(x):$  $x$ is divisible by $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414642 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9889c22d-72d2-5a8b-9484-672aacb03351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414642 #edb/ref 1074 "The given sentence is equivalent to the following sentence: \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not divisible by $2$, and $x$ is not divisible by $3.$\n\nLet's break our sentence down into individual statements:\n\n- The statement \"$x$ is not divisible by $2$\" can be expressed symbolically as $¬P.$\n\n- The statement \"$x$ is not divisible by $3$\" can be expressed symbolically as $¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n¬P∧¬Q.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
